<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Obizzo III d'Este</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1294">1294</a>–<a href="/years/1352">1352</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-este">House of Este</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Obizzo III d'Este was marquis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> from 1317 and lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> from 1336, strengthening Este rule over both cities. He acquired Parma in 1344 but, unable to defend it, sold it to Luchino Visconti of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1346. His son Niccolò II and later descendants continued the Este dynasty in Ferrara.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/obizzo-iii-deste.jpg" alt="Obizzo III d'Este" width="286" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Obizzo_III_d%27Este2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Obizzo was born on 14 July 1294, the son of Aldobrandino II d'Este and Alda Rangoni. He became Marquess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> in 1317, but at first he did not rule alone: the lordship of Ferrara was shared with his brothers Rinaldo and Niccolò and with his cousin Folco II. Obizzo eventually became the sole ruler of Ferrara, and he kept the title of marquess until his death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Obizzo expanded the possessions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-este">Este family</a> beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> by acquiring the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> and Parma. In 1336 the general council of the commune and people of Modena restored the family to the lordship of that city, and in 1344 Parma was sold to him. Ferrara remained the seat of his rule until he died on 20 March 1352.</p><p>In May 1317 Obizzo married Giacoma Pepoli, daughter of Romeo de' Peppoli of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>; the marriage was childless. There are also reports that he married Princess Elisabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-ii-duke-of-saxony">Albert II, Duke of Saxony</a>. His long-time mistress was Lippa Ariosti, known as la Bella, with whom he had many children. He married her in 1347, shortly before her death on 27 November that year, so that their children would be legitimate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Of the sons of Obizzo and Lippa, Rinaldo, Azzo and Folco died young, while Aldobrandino III, Niccolò II and Alberto survived their father. The daughters married into ruling families: Alisia to Guido III Novello da Polenta, Lord of Ravenna, in 1349; Alda to Ludovico II Gonzaga in 1356; Costanza to Malatesta IV Malatesta, Lord of Rimini, in 1363; and Beatrice to Waldemar I, Prince of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zerbst">Zerbst</a>, in 1365. Obizzo also had an illegitimate son by an unknown mistress, Giovanni, later Governor of Frignano, who was likewise legitimised.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Obizzo III d'Este” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a succession of lords, <b>Obizzo III d'Este</b> sold the city to Luchino Visconti in 1346, and it joined the Lordship of Milan.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-parma">Commune of Parma</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.84,11.62&amp;year=1331" aria-label="Ferrara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Obizzo III d'Este"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5700 3710h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5700 3710h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.84,11.62&amp;year=1331">What was here in 1331? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
